{"id":"8b0312af-3f44-4048-85c2-5df4188eb7ac","object":"chat.completion","created":1784958199,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if 'threshold' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing threshold\\\")\\n    threshold = case['threshold']\\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"threshold must be a number\\\")\\n    if 'calls' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing calls\\\")\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"calls must be a list\\\")\\n    accepted = []\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    for call in calls:\\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"each call must be a dict\\\")\\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\\n        for key in required:\\n            if key not in call:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Missing {key} in call\\\")\\n        call_id = call['call_id']\\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"call_id must be string\\\")\\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_without_call must be number\\\")\\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"loss_with_call must be number\\\")\\n        pred_tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens <= 0:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"prediction_tokens must be positive integer\\\")\\n        parseable = call['parseable']\\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"parseable must be boolean\\\")\\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\\n        accepted_flag = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted_flag,\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": round(normalized_gain, 8)\\n        })\\n        if accepted_flag:\\n            accepted.append(call_id)\\n    return {\\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted, \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task AGENT-TF-01 based on the description. The task: \"api_call_loss_filter\" with bounded objective: retain API-call candidates using normalized future-token weights, weighted losses, stronger counterfactual, inclusive threshold. The description says:\n\n- For each call subtract loss with the call from loss without it: gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call.\n- Normalize gain by at least one prediction token: normalized_gain = gain / prediction_tokens. But careful: \"normalize gain by at least one prediction token\" likely means divide by prediction_tokens, but if prediction_tokens is 0? It's given in fixtures as positive integer (2,3,4,5,6). So we can divide.\n- Require both parseable true and normalized_gain >= threshold (threshold from case).\n- Keep diagnostics in supplied call order.\n- Return accepted_call_ids (list of call_ids that satisfy condition) and diagnostics (list of dicts with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain) for each call (all calls, not just accepted? The output schema for diagnostics: array of objects with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain. It doesn't say only for accepted, so likely for all calls in order. Pay attention: the diagnostics array must have same length as calls? Might be required. In examples in the payload, but output schema doesn't specify required array length. However, \"Keep diagnostics in supplied call order\" implies we should produce diagnostics for each call in that order.\n- Normalized_gain should be a number (float). The output schema says type \"number\" (JSON number). Should we round? \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So we need to round normalized_gain to some precision? The description: \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" No explicit decimal places. In the fixtures, values are floats with many decimals. Likely we should round to a reasonable number, e.g., 6 decimal places or maybe as is? The instruction \"Return only JSON-serializable values\" - floats are OK. But \"rounded\" suggests we should round to some precision. However, there is no specification of precision. Common practice: round to 6 decimal places or maybe the same as input? But the input has 8 decimals. To be safe, we can round to 8 decimals? But typical test might check equality. Since the instruction says \"rounded\", we could round to 8 decimal places or maybe 6? Let's check the description: \"rounded per-call diagnostics\" - possibly they just want to avoid floating-point artifacts. We can compute normalized_gain and round to, say, 8 decimal places. Or we could compute and not round, but that might cause floating point issues. Usually in such tasks, they expect exactly the computed value, maybe not rounded. But \"rounded\" suggests rounding. I'll round to 8 decimal places using round(value, 8). That should be fine.\n\nAlso note: \"stronger counterfactual\" - not sure if there is any other condition. The description says \"using normalized future-token weights, weighted losses, the stronger counterfactual\". Wait, there is mention of \"normalized_future_token_weights\" and \"weighted losses\" but the payload does not have such fields. The payload has loss_without_call, loss_with_call, prediction_tokens, parseable. There is no \"weight\" field. The \"counterfactual\" is essentially the gain (loss_without_call - loss_with_call). \"stronger counterfactual\" might mean we use that gain directly. No additional weighting. So just compute normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens.\n\nCheck conditions: parseable == True and normalized_gain >= threshold.\n\nThreshold is given as 0.05 in fixtures.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Extract threshold and calls.\n- Initialize accepted = [] and diagnostics = [].\n- For each call in order:\n  - compute gain = call['loss_without_call'] - call['loss_with_call']\n  - normalized_gain = gain / call['prediction_tokens']\n  - accepted_flag = call['parseable'] and normalized_gain >= threshold\n  - append to diagnostics: {\"accepted\": accepted_flag, \"call_id\": call['call_id'], \"normalized_gain\": round(normalized_gain, 8)}\n  - if accepted_flag, append call['call_id'] to accepted.\n- Return dict with \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted and \"diagnostics\": diagnostics.\n\nWe must ensure we do not mutate input.\n\nAlso note: The task says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So if case is missing keys or has wrong types, we raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation: define solve(case) that takes a dict. Validate that 'threshold' is present and numeric, 'calls' is list. For each call, validate required fields: call_id (string), loss_without_call (number), loss_with_call (number), prediction_tokens (integer > 0), parseable (bool). If any missing or invalid, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso ensure that prediction_tokens > 0 to avoid division by zero. If zero, raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce JSON output as described.\n\nThe output must be a JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing Python source as a string. So we need to write the code as a string, escaping appropriately.\n\nWe'll write a clean solve function.\n\nPotential edge cases: threshold could be negative? Not specified. But we use >=.\n\nLet's produce the code.\n\nWe'll define:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate threshold\n    if 'threshold' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing threshold\")\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    if not isinstance(threshold, (int, float)):\n        raise ValueError(\"threshold must be a number\")\n    if 'calls' not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing calls\")\n    calls = case['calls']\n    if not isinstance(calls, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"calls must be a list\")\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for call in calls:\n        if not isinstance(call, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each call must be a dict\")\n        # validate fields\n        required = ['call_id', 'loss_without_call', 'loss_with_call', 'prediction_tokens', 'parseable']\n        for key in required:\n            if key not in call:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Missing {key} in call\")\n        call_id = call['call_id']\n        if not isinstance(call_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"call_id must be string\")\n        loss_without = call['loss_without_call']\n        if not isinstance(loss_without, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_without_call must be number\")\n        loss_with = call['loss_with_call']\n        if not isinstance(loss_with, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"loss_with_call must be number\")\n        pred_tokens = call['prediction_tokens']\n        if not isinstance(pred_tokens, int) or pred_tokens <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"prediction_tokens must be positive integer\")\n        parseable = call['parseable']\n        if not isinstance(parseable, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"parseable must be boolean\")\n        gain = loss_without - loss_with\n        normalized_gain = gain / pred_tokens\n        accepted_flag = parseable and (normalized_gain >= threshold)\n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"accepted\": accepted_flag,\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": round(normalized_gain, 8)  # round to 8 decimal places\n        })\n        if accepted_flag:\n            accepted.append(call_id)\n    return {\"accepted_call_ids\": accepted, \"diagnostics\": diagnostics}\n\nWe need to ensure we don't mutate case. The function doesn't mutate.\n\nNow wrap in JSON with escaping.\n\nFinal response should be a single JSON object with \"implementation\" key.\n\nWe'll output that."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2171,"completion_tokens":2242,"total_tokens":4413,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1691},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":123},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}